{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>For Leah was hated</b> — Now Leah deceived her sister as well as Jacob, for even if we say that she acted out of honor for her father who grabbed hold of her and brought her in to him and told her not to reveal it, she should have told him or hinted to him that she was Leah, even though she kept herself disguised all night so that he did not recognize her until he saw her in the morning, and therefore Jacob hated her. But Elohim knew that she did this only in order to be married to the righteous man, and He had compassion on her. Thus they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 71:2): When Jacob saw the deeds — that Leah had deceived her sister — he set his mind to divorce her, but when the Holy One, blessed be He, visited her with children, he said: Shall I divorce the mother of these? And this is the meaning of \"And Elohim saw\" — for He had compassion on her so that he would not abandon her. And some say (Radak) that with two women where one is loved very much, the other is called \"hated\" by contrast, as it says, \"And he loved Rachel more than Leah\" — not that he hated her, and she was ashamed of the matter, and Elohim saw her affliction."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {0 "The phrase ואל תמר בו is read as 'and he told her not to reveal it' based on the context of Leah's silence at her father's instruction; the verb תמר (to change/substitute) is taken idiomatically here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:36:36.360691617Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}